From 72b6d8ebe6a23ab41e0634a334a71c73f0897596 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 03:50:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 11/24] Validate the kernel walk as a sandwich, and refuse what
 QEMU would fault

Runtime validation compared a CPU read with a later software read, so a
guest store in between, such as the page-table entries being wired, read
as a disagreement and retired a correct walker until the root changed.
Each sample is now CPU, walk, CPU again: bytes that moved make it
inconclusive, counting toward nothing and revoking nothing. A stable
disagreement is likely real but not proven under concurrency, so it
takes two to retire a root.

A capture that cannot read the translation state now takes the held root
out of service until a later capture validates again, instead of leaving
a possibly stale snapshot serving.

The walk checked only the final output address against TCR.IPS. Like
QEMU's walker, it now refuses a root table or any next-level table
beyond the output size before reading it, and refuses the reserved IPS
encoding.

Validation is bounded so it moves the timing it studies less: a reader
takes at most sixteen samples while its root validates and one audit
sample after, one boundary probe per reader while a crossing is still
owed, and the probe reads 128 bytes each side so a non-zero crossing
comes sooner. Samples and probes are counted in store_routes and a
kva_walk_cost line reports each boot's totals at its reset.

